// Bidirectional `rewrite`: an equation cited in the "wrong" orientation for the
// goal still works, without a preceding `symmetry` flip. Here `@b-equals-a` is
// `B = A`, and we rewrite the target `B = C` to `A = C` — that needs replacing
// `B` (the equation's LEFT... no: RIGHT side) with `A`. The kernel now tries both
// `lhs->rhs` and `rhs->lhs`, so `[by rewrite b-equals-a b-equals-c]` derives
// `A = C` even though `B = A` is oriented rhs->lhs relative to the substitution.
sort Thing
const A: Thing
const B: Thing
const C: Thing
axiom bEqA: B = A
axiom bEqC: B = C

// A = C, by rewriting B->A in `B = C` using the equation `B = A` cited as-is
// (its lhs B is what we replace, giving A) — this is the FORWARD case, kept as a
// control that both directions still handle the ordinary orientation.
theorem forwardControl: A = C
proof
  @b-equals-a |
    B = A
    [by cite bEqA]
  @b-equals-c |
    B = C
    [by cite bEqC]
  @conclusion |
    A = C
    [by rewrite b-equals-a b-equals-c]
qed

// The REVERSE case: cite `A = B` (call it aEqB) and rewrite the target `B = C`.
// To turn `B = C` into `A = C` we must replace `B` with `A` — but `A = B` has B
// on its RIGHT. The directional kernel would need `[by symmetry]` first; the
// bidirectional kernel accepts it directly via the rhs->lhs orientation.
axiom aEqB: A = B

theorem reverseCase: A = C
proof
  @a-equals-b |
    A = B
    [by cite aEqB]
  @b-equals-c |
    B = C
    [by cite bEqC]
  @conclusion |
    A = C
    [by rewrite a-equals-b b-equals-c]
qed
